[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"PHP Code Style Guides: Ensuring Consistency and Readability"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Maintaining a consistent code style is crucial for any PHP project, especially when working in teams. A well-defined code style guide enhances readability, reduces errors, and simplifies maintenance. This tutorial outlines key aspects of PHP code style guides, drawing from widely accepted standards like PSR-1, PSR-2, and PSR-12."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"PSR Standards: The Foundation of PHP Code Style"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The PHP Standards Recommendations (PSR) are a set of specifications published by the PHP Framework Interop Group (PHP-FIG). They provide guidelines for various aspects of PHP development, including coding style. PSR-1, PSR-2, and PSR-12 are particularly relevant to code style."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"PSR-1: Basic Coding Standard"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Files MUST use only <?php and <?= tags."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Files MUST be encoded in UTF-8 without BOM."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Classes, functions, and constants MUST be declared with proper PHP syntax."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Namespaces and classes MUST follow an "autoloading" standard (PSR-4 is recommended)."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Class names MUST be declared in StudlyCaps."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Constant names MUST be declared in all upper case with underscore separators."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Method names MUST be declared in camelCase."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"PSR-2: Coding Style Guide"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Code MUST use 4 spaces for indenting, not tabs."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lines SHOULD NOT be longer than 80 characters; lines MUST NOT be longer than 120 characters."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"There MUST be one blank line after the namespace declaration."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"There MUST be one blank line after the class opening brace."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Properties and methods MUST be declared with one visibility; abstract and final MUST precede the visibility; static MUST come after the visibility."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Control structure keywords MUST have one space after them; method and function calls MUST NOT."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Opening braces for classes and methods MUST go on their own line; closing braces MUST go on their own line."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"PSR-12: Extended Coding Style Guide"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"PSR-12 is an evolution of PSR-2, providing more specific and modern guidelines. It clarifies and expands upon the rules defined in PSR-2, addressing aspects like argument lists, control structures, and more."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Adds rules for short list syntax."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Clarifies rules for control structures (e.g., 'if', 'for', 'while')."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides more specific guidance on the use of whitespace."},{"order":26,"type":"subheading","text":"Example of PSR Compliant Code"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"<?php

namespace My\Namespace;

use Some\Other\ClassA as ClassB;

interface MyInterface
{
    public function myFunction($arg1, $arg2);
}

abstract class MyAbstractClass implements MyInterface
{
    protected static $myStaticProperty;

    abstract protected function myAbstractMethod();

    final public function myFunction($arg1, $arg2)
    {
        if ($arg1 === $arg2) {
            // ...
        } elseif ($arg1 > $arg2) {
            return $this->myAbstractMethod();
        } else {
            return ClassB::staticMethod($arg1, $arg2);
        }
    }
}
"},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Tools for Enforcing Code Style"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Several tools can help automate the process of enforcing code style in PHP projects:"},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PHP_CodeSniffer: A tool that tokenizes PHP, JavaScript and CSS files and detects violations of a defined set of coding standards."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PHP-CS-Fixer: A tool to automatically fix PHP coding standards issues."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Psalm/PHPStan: Static analysis tools that can also detect code style issues and enforce best practices."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuration Examples"},{"order":34,"type":"code","text":"// Example .php-cs-fixer.dist.php configuration
<?php

$finder = PhpCsFixer\Finder::create()
    ->in(__DIR__)
    ->exclude('var');

$config = new PhpCsFixer\Config();
return $config
    ->setRules([
        '@PSR12' => true,
        'strict_param' => true,
        'array_syntax' => ['syntax' => 'short'],
    ])
    ->setFinder($finder);
"},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Using a Code Style Guide"},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Readability: Consistent code is easier to understand and maintain."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced Errors: A clear style guide helps prevent common coding mistakes."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Easier Collaboration: Teams can work together more effectively when everyone follows the same style."},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simplified Maintenance: Consistent code is easier to refactor and update."},{"order":40,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"Adopting a PHP code style guide, such as those based on PSR standards, is essential for creating maintainable, readable, and collaborative projects. By using tools to enforce these standards, developers can ensure consistency and improve the overall quality of their code."}]
